WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:06.090
In a car park, a display tells drivers how many bays are free.

00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:10.210
More and more, that count comes from sensors, not an attendant's tally.

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:14.686
A poster from the School of Continuous Improvement asks what happens

00:00:14.886 --> 00:00:19.286
when the operator's bonus is tied to how fast disputes are closed.

00:00:20.186 --> 00:00:22.936
We ran SLOT for eighteen weeks, across ten car parks.

00:00:23.636 --> 00:00:27.286
Each bay has a magnetometer puck that reports whether it's free.

00:00:28.186 --> 00:00:28.966
Take one bay.

00:00:29.166 --> 00:00:32.026
Its sensor calls it free, but the call is disputed.

00:00:32.326 --> 00:00:33.696
A flag goes into a queue,

00:00:33.896 --> 00:00:35.766
a duty officer confirms or denies it,

00:00:35.966 --> 00:00:37.396
and the flag is closed,

00:00:37.596 --> 00:00:39.314
every step logged to the second.

00:00:40.214 --> 00:00:44.204
Then the bonus began paying on how fast each flag closed.

00:00:44.504 --> 00:00:47.964
Nothing in the contract paid on whether the bay was actually free.

00:00:48.864 --> 00:00:52.764
Before the bonus, a disputed call took a median of forty minutes to close.

00:00:53.064 --> 00:00:55.744
Seven and a half minutes, once the bonus was paying.

00:00:56.644 --> 00:01:00.734
On the chart, every car park moves from the black dot, before the bonus,

00:01:00.934 --> 00:01:02.614
to the gold dot, after it.

00:01:03.014 --> 00:01:05.704
But the accuracy of the calls didn't change.

00:01:06.604 --> 00:01:09.584
The dashboard doesn't need to know why the number moved,

00:01:09.784 --> 00:01:10.694
only that it did.

00:01:11.994 --> 00:01:17.874
Next, a follow-on trial ties part of the bonus to a blinded spot-check of closed calls.

00:01:18.774 --> 00:01:21.224
A bonus finds whatever it's pointed at.

00:01:21.524 --> 00:01:23.104
Ours was pointed at the clock.
